# Build output
target/
dist/
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Local environment / secrets (never committed - see infra/.env.example)
#
# The .* variants matter as much as the plain ones: rotating a secret leaves a .env.bak, and
# editors leave .env.swp and .env~. Only `.env` was listed here, so any of those would have been
# an untracked file holding a live credential, waiting for the next `git add -A`.
# .env.example is the committed template and is excluded from the exclusion.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
infra/.env
infra/.env.*
!infra/.env.example

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store
*.swp
# Editor backups. `.env.*` above does not catch `.env~` - no dot - and that file holds whatever
# the secret was before the last edit.
*~

# Participant data. Never committed.
#
# The exports carry Prolific ids, which are pseudonymous personal data under the ethics
# application: a repository is the wrong place for them, and git history is the wrong place
# twice over - a commit that lands here is still there after the file is deleted, and this
# repository is on GitHub.
#
# Put the Qualtrics and platform exports under data/ and work on them there. The code that
# reads them is committed; what they contain is not. The anonymised derivative (ids replaced
# with P001, P002, ...) is the one that may be shared, and it is not excepted here on purpose:
# decide that deliberately, per file, rather than by a pattern that looked safe once.
# A directory named data/ at any level holds them, and nothing in it is committed except the
# README that says so. `!` cannot rescue a file inside an ignored DIRECTORY, so the contents are
# excluded rather than the directory itself.
**/data/*
!**/data/README.md

# And a blanket rule, because the export does not have to be put in the right place to be
# dangerous - a CSV dropped in the repository root is the likelier accident. Nothing tracked
# today is a .csv, so this excludes no existing file.
*.csv
